  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 500 AM EDT FRI SEP 25 2026\n\nThe beginning of this period will still be in the northeast flow \nregime in the lower levels and northerly flow at 500 mb resulting\nin drier and pleasant weather. That said, the northeast flow \nresults from being upwind of a strong surface low off the New \nEngland coast and between that low and the axis a ridge of sfc \nhigh pressure extending from Quebec across the Great Lakes and\ninto the TN and OH Valleys. This flow will keep highs roughly 5 \nto 10 degrees below normal for late September, in the upper 60s to\nlow 70s for Sunday. Our positioning relative to the New England \nlow, the upper level ridging extending across the central US to \nthe Great Lakes, and sfc high pressure extending into the Lower OH\nValley and Commonwealth will keep eastern KY dry for the rest of \nthe weekend as well. Fog along the river valleys will be likely \nSaturday and Sunday Night, but otherwise skies should remain \nmostly clear for the remainder of the weekend. It's also worth \nmentioning that valley inversions/ridge valley temperature splits \nshould be present Saturday night and Sunday Night, with lows in \nthe low to mid 40s in the valleys and upper 40s to low 50s on the \nridges.\n\nThe start of the week will also be pleasant with the previously \nmentioned upper ridge in the central US migrating east into the \nregion as the sfc and upper low initially over the Mid Atlantic \nstates departs to the northeast. This ridge and associated sfc \nhigh pressure will keep conditions dry for the first half of the\nweek and also help bring about a gradual warming trend. Expect \nhighs on Monday to be in the low to mid 70s and warming each day \nuntil the 80s return around Wednesday. Ensembles show broad \ntroughing from the central US at midweek, while the axis of\nridging shifting east. This results in some low end pops as early\nas Wednesday. PoPs look to be persistent through the end of the \nwork week and beyond given uncertainty in the evolution of this \ntrough and sfc features. Daily rainfall chances from Wednesday \ninto next weekend does make sense given the broad and elongated \ntrough that both ensemble and deterministic guidance is suggesting.\nThis troughing pattern will bring west to southwesterly surface \nflow and eventually some Gulf moisture which will allow >60 \ndegree dewpoints to move in mid-late week. These higher dewpoints\nwill bring a notable increase in humidity as well aside from  \nrain chances. All in all, expect the return of warmth and\neventually some rain by the end of next week.",
